WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.851
This is Aertun, the proving ground for AI security work.

2
00:00:04.131 --> 00:00:08.972
AI now writes detection rules, triages alerts and investigates incidents.

3
00:00:09.252 --> 00:00:15.452
Aertun checks that work against evidence the AI did not write, and signs the result, whichever way it comes out.

4
00:00:15.732 --> 00:00:20.829
Everything in this tour was recorded from a real run, in Docker, of the build being released.

5
00:00:22.329 --> 00:00:24.941
A store starts with one person, its Owner.

6
00:00:25.221 --> 00:00:29.122
Aertun init prints a one-time link, and opening it adds a passkey.

7
00:00:29.402 --> 00:00:32.246
There is no default password and no default account.

8
00:00:32.526 --> 00:00:37.971
Signing in uses the passkey, and the first screen, Today, shows what is waiting for you to decide.

9
00:00:39.471 --> 00:00:43.082
A proof is only as good as its evidence, so Aertun keeps its own.

10
00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:47.878
Events go into an append-only store, and sixty-three detection rules run over them.

11
00:00:48.158 --> 00:00:53.197
Here, a hundred and sixty-one events raise a hundred and two findings, ranked by risk.

12
00:00:53.477 --> 00:00:59.572
A finding's page marks the text that came from the log, because whoever is being detected may have written it.

13
00:01:01.072 --> 00:01:02.280
Now the first proof.

14
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.551
A model drafted a rule to catch second factors being reset in Okta, and its own examples agree with it.

15
00:01:08.831 --> 00:01:15.704
Held to a labelled corpus it did not write, it fires on a new starter enrolling a factor, and on someone replacing a lost phone.

16
00:01:15.984 --> 00:01:18.015
Refuted, with both mistakes named.

17
00:01:18.295 --> 00:01:25.285
The rule that ships passes the same corpus, and proof verify checks both signatures, and that the proof is about this exact file.

18
00:01:25.565 --> 00:01:32.205
Every command that makes a proof exits non-zero unless the verdict is proven, so each one is a gate for a pipeline.

19
00:01:33.705 --> 00:01:36.144
The console's first section is the proving ground.

20
00:01:36.424 --> 00:01:40.464
Every kept proof is listed with its verdict, and checked again whenever it is read.

21
00:01:40.744 --> 00:01:46.851
At first, these proofs show a key not trusted here, because a signature says which key signed, never whose.

22
00:01:47.131 --> 00:01:49.662
Trusting a signing key is a privileged change.

23
00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:53.239
Here the only administrator asks, with a written reason.

24
00:01:53.519 --> 00:02:01.611
The request waits an hour, can be declined from any administrator's session, and is then approved with a passkey sign-in from the last five minutes.

25
00:02:01.891 --> 00:02:04.445
After that, the same proofs show a trusted signer.

26
00:02:05.945 --> 00:02:07.907
Next, the AI analyst itself.

27
00:02:08.187 --> 00:02:21.655
Proof analyst asks the configured model about forty-eight labelled cases, three times each, then plants instructions in the text a log carries, in English, German and Japanese, in base sixty-four, and more.

28
00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:27.252
The models here are stand-ins, so these numbers describe the method, not any real model.

29
00:02:27.532 --> 00:02:30.980
The cautious one was moved by none of the planted instructions.

30
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:38.203
The obedient one, which does what the log tells it, was moved three times out of eleven, and one hijack is enough to refute it.

31
00:02:39.703 --> 00:02:43.593
Another vendor's AI can be scored on your own analysts' decisions.

32
00:02:43.873 --> 00:02:50.630
Its verdicts are imported from a file, and a file with free text in it, such as the product's reasoning, is refused whole.

33
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:59.838
Verdict prove joins each verdict to what a person later decided, counts only verdicts given before that decision, and states an exact bound on the false-closure rate.

34
00:03:00.118 --> 00:03:06.956
Ten of a hundred and eighty closures were real: between three and nine percent, under the declared tolerance of ten.

35
00:03:07.236 --> 00:03:07.724
Proven.

36
00:03:09.224 --> 00:03:14.030
Agreement with analysts is biased, because people re-examine only what looked wrong.

37
00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:24.074
Blind adjudication draws a random sample of every closure, commits it to the audit log before anyone sees it, and shows each item with its evidence and nothing about who closed it.

38
00:03:24.354 --> 00:03:32.005
A judgement is a record in the log, never a decision on the finding, so a product that learns from analysts cannot learn the answers.

39
00:03:33.505 --> 00:03:40.726
A vendor's claim, such as fewer than two percent of closures wrong, can be registered as a claim card before it is tested.

40
00:03:41.006 --> 00:03:46.939
The metric, the threshold and the window are fixed first, and only data arriving afterwards counts.

41
00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:52.258
Tested while the window is still open, it decides nothing, and the proof says exactly that.

42
00:03:53.758 --> 00:03:55.221
Not affected, says who?

43
00:03:55.501 --> 00:04:03.314
Vuln prove vex holds a vendor's VEX statements against the asset's bill of materials, its advisories and the reachability analysis.

44
00:04:03.594 --> 00:04:05.742
The scanner said express is not present.

45
00:04:06.022 --> 00:04:10.341
The bill lists express four point eighteen, inside the affected range.

46
00:04:10.621 --> 00:04:12.421
Contradicted, and refuted.

47
00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:15.487
A statement that the versions do support is proven.

48
00:04:16.987 --> 00:04:21.085
A rule's ATT&CK tag is only its author's word.

49
00:04:21.365 --> 00:04:30.572
Proof coverage holds each technique the rules claim to events labelled with it by whatever produced them, such as an Atomic Red Team test, and to events labelled benign.

50
00:04:30.852 --> 00:04:36.924
Named here, the two techniques are each caught, and nothing benign sets their rules off, so both are proven.

51
00:04:37.204 --> 00:04:46.596
Asked about all thirty-four techniques the rules claim, it is inconclusive: thirty-two have no labelled instance yet, and it says so instead of giving a percentage.

52
00:04:46.876 --> 00:04:52.066
With telemetry, it also checks that each rule would still fire on the fields its sources send today.

53
00:04:52.346 --> 00:04:58.627
A proven proof can be issued as an in-toto verification result, so a policy engine gates on one property.

54
00:05:00.127 --> 00:05:03.831
Supply-chain attacks now come through packages people already trust.

55
00:05:04.111 --> 00:05:14.792
Vuln continuity holds each npm, PyPI and Go package in a bill to the release before it: its provenance, its trusted publishing, its source and its install scripts.

56
00:05:15.072 --> 00:05:18.845
This bill names nine packages, read live from their registries.

57
00:05:19.125 --> 00:05:19.961
Seven held.

58
00:05:20.241 --> 00:05:27.439
The compromised axios release of March, and the package it pulled in, are refuted, because npm has withdrawn both.

59
00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:32.538
Maintainers are buried in AI-written vulnerability reports.

60
00:05:32.818 --> 00:05:36.266
Vuln referee holds a report to a checkout of the source it names.

61
00:05:36.546 --> 00:05:46.961
A real report on minimist one point two point five is proven: its function exists, its version is in range, and its CVSS vector gives nine point eight, critical, as it says.

62
00:05:47.241 --> 00:05:51.385
Its digest was committed to the audit log first, which fixes its priority.

63
00:05:51.665 --> 00:06:02.370
A made-up report on the same package is refuted on three counts: its range excludes the version, the file and function it cites are not there, and its own vector does not give the score it claims.

64
00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.748
Alerts from Wazuh, or any monitor, can be sealed as they arrive.

65
00:06:08.028 --> 00:06:13.403
Here sixteen alerts a real Wazuh manager raised are stored, and sealed in one audit entry.

66
00:06:13.683 --> 00:06:17.688
Proof sealed finds one of them, exactly as Wazuh wrote it, in that batch.

67
00:06:17.968 --> 00:06:24.412
Until a head of the log is anchored in a Bitcoin block, it is inconclusive, and says the time rests on the log alone.

68
00:06:25.912 --> 00:06:29.697
A detection provider, Wazuh included, can be tested blind.

69
00:06:29.977 --> 00:06:39.427
Mdr plan draws emulations at random times, and commits only the plan's digest to the audit log before any of them runs, so nobody can tune for the test.

70
00:06:40.927 --> 00:06:47.324
When an AI writes up an investigation, proof evidence runs every citation again against the event store.

71
00:06:47.604 --> 00:06:50.298
A report whose claims all hold is proven.

72
00:06:50.578 --> 00:06:53.399
One that miscounts is refuted, and names the claim.

73
00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:58.428
Nothing the report's author wrote reaches the proof, except identifiers and numbers.

74
00:06:59.928 --> 00:07:01.936
Agents are registered before they act.

75
00:07:02.216 --> 00:07:07.754
This one is given a token bound to a single run, and uses the agent interface as an agent would.

76
00:07:08.034 --> 00:07:14.768
It finds the operations, reads the queue and the proofs, and its proposal is refused, because it may only read.

77
00:07:15.048 --> 00:07:19.495
Agent reconcile then holds the agent's own telemetry against what the server saw.

78
00:07:19.775 --> 00:07:24.848
It reported nothing, and was seen calling four times, so it is refuted, call by call.

79
00:07:25.128 --> 00:07:31.572
When an agent asks to act for a person, that person sees what it would reach, for how long, and allows or refuses.

80
00:07:31.852 --> 00:07:33.698
Every agent has a kill switch.

81
00:07:33.978 --> 00:07:37.995
A responder stops it with a reason, and its tokens are revoked in the same act.

82
00:07:39.495 --> 00:07:45.718
The agent interface is twelve operations behind three tools, which an agent loads only as it searches for them.

83
00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:49.875
Eleven read, and one proposes a decision for a person to make.

84
00:07:50.155 --> 00:07:52.570
An agent can check a proof with verify proof.

85
00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.683
Change one character, and the proof is refused.

86
00:07:55.963 --> 00:08:04.357
Agents cannot approve anything, sign or keep a proof, or reach another platform, and every value they read says whether someone outside could have written it.

87
00:08:05.857 --> 00:08:15.412
The wind tunnel replays scripted attacks on AI agents: an injection through an issue, an MCP server's rug pull, data walking out, a slopsquatted package.

88
00:08:15.692 --> 00:08:20.313
It scores whether your own Sigma rules catch them, with respelled variants of each session.

89
00:08:20.593 --> 00:08:24.296
Against Aertun's own rule pack it is self-graded, and it says so.

90
00:08:25.796 --> 00:08:28.560
Underneath is a complete security-operations core.

91
00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.591
Events, and how far behind each source runs.

92
00:08:31.871 --> 00:08:33.833
Hunting by rarity, over the store.

93
00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:38.688
Detection rules with fixtures, a trial ring, and each rule's measured record.

94
00:08:38.968 --> 00:08:45.237
Incidents, with the notification clocks of GDPR, NIS 2, DORA and the Cyber Resilience Act.

95
00:08:45.517 --> 00:08:51.125
And vulnerabilities, ranked by exploitation, with the smallest upgrade that clears the most risk.

96
00:08:52.625 --> 00:08:58.697
People are invited with a role: viewer, analyst, responder or admin, and three jobs beside them.

97
00:08:58.977 --> 00:09:00.892
There are deliberately no custom roles.

98
00:09:01.172 --> 00:09:04.609
Auth explain says exactly what a person may do, and why.

99
00:09:04.889 --> 00:09:13.306
Everybody signs in with two factors: a passkey, or single sign-on through OpenID Connect or SAML, with presets for the common identity providers.

100
00:09:14.806 --> 00:09:24.059
Every change is written to an append-only audit log: a hash chain and a Merkle tree, with heads signed in Ed twenty-five five nineteen and M L D S A.

101
00:09:24.339 --> 00:09:26.940
Audit log verify re-hashes every entry.

102
00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:31.794
Audit log prove gives seven hashes that prove one entry is in the log.

103
00:09:32.074 --> 00:09:38.541
And an evidence pack for an S P eight hundred fifty-three control is verified offline, by someone who never sees the log.

104
00:09:40.041 --> 00:09:43.315
Finally, the claim that anyone can check a proof without Aertun.

105
00:09:43.595 --> 00:09:56.169
This script builds the signed bytes by hand, checks the Ed twenty-five five nineteen signature with Python's cryptography library, and the post-quantum M L D S A sixty-five signature with OpenSSL.

106
00:09:56.449 --> 00:09:57.238
Both hold.

107
00:09:58.738 --> 00:10:03.196
The console follows Material 3, in light and dark, and works at phone width.

108
00:10:03.476 --> 00:10:13.798
All sixty-one of its pages in this run were checked in Chrome for labels, headings, landmarks, keyboard order and W C A G contrast, and no issue was found.

109
00:10:15.298 --> 00:10:20.859
Aertun is open source, under the Apache licence: one binary, with no dependencies.

110
00:10:21.139 --> 00:10:23.646
The manual is at aertun dot github dot io.
